-\subsection{Related work: two literatures, and the question between them}
+\subsection{Related work}
 \label{sec:related}
+De novo generators including EDM, GeoLDM, SemlaFlow and FlowMol3
+\citep{edm,geoldm,semlaflow,flowmol3} learn molecular composition and geometry jointly. Conditional
+conformer and energy-targeted samplers address a different, fixed-system problem
+\citep{torsionaldiff,noe2019boltzmann,fab,adjointsampling,idem,ewfm}.
+Adjoint Sampling uses neural energy models across molecular systems
+\citep{adjointsampling}. Log-variance objectives, partition-constant cancellation and off-policy
+reference measures are prior work \citep{vargrad,nusken2021pathspace,richter2024improvedsampling,sendera2024offpolicy}.
+In particular, off-policy log-dispersion regularisation already combines data-based training with
+energy-labelled shape regularisation without additional on-policy samples \citep{ldr2026}.
+We claim neither the divergence nor that combination as new. The question here is its implementation
+and empirical behaviour in a bond-free molecular FlowMol3 model; broader related work is in
+\Cref{app:related}.
 
-\paragraph{De novo 3D molecular generation.} Equivariant diffusion and flow-matching models
-generate positions and types jointly, from EDM and GeoLDM \citep{edm,geoldm} through
-graph-and-geometry hybrids \citep{midi,symphony,semlaflow} to FlowMol3 \citep{flowmol3}, the
-backbone used here, and Zatom-1 \citep{zatom1}, the one published generator trained on the same
-corpus; EBMol \citep{ebmol} learns a scalar energy from data rather than from a physical potential.
-These models are judged on validity, on sample quality, or on an energy the model itself learns, and
-they define the state of the art at the task they set out to do. What such evaluations generally do
-not ask is whether the tractable density the model defines responds to an \emph{external} physical
-energy. That is a different question rather than a shortfall --- it is not the question these papers
-pose --- and we claim superiority over none of them.
+\subsection{Backbone and the ideal density}
+\label{sec:method:flow}
+A composition $c$ specifies atom types and total charge, and coordinates lie in the zero-centroid
+subspace $H$ of dimension $3(N-1)$. The backbone is FlowMol3 on OMol25 with no bond supervision
+and at most $200$ atoms. The energy teacher supplies values in eV, with $kT=1$ eV a numerical
+scale. Spin is fixed to a singlet and charge information is restricted by preprocessing; the
+reported energy evaluations concern main-group organic parents.
 
-\paragraph{Conditional conformer and ensemble models.} Torsional diffusion \citep{torsionaldiff},
-the GEOM conformer literature \citep{geom} and DECAF \citep{decaf} take the molecular graph, or the
-molecular identity, as input and return conformers for it. The composition is supplied rather than
-generated, so the density they define is conditional on a fixed chemical graph. That input is what
-makes theirs a different problem from a de novo generator's, and why their protocols do not transfer
-to one unchanged.
+FlowMol3's position head predicts an endpoint $D_\theta(x,t)$, not a velocity
+\citep{flowmol3}. For the position schedule $\alpha$, the smooth position velocity is
+\begin{equation}
+ v_\theta(x,t)=\frac{\alpha'(t)}{1-\alpha(t)}\bigl(D_\theta(x,t)-x\bigr).
+ \label{eq:headvelocity}
+\end{equation}
+For a deterministic field projected as $v_H(x,t)=P v_\theta(Px,t)$, fixed discrete labels, and an
+interval where the flow is well-defined and invertible, change of variables gives
+\begin{equation}
+ \log q_T(x\mid c)=\log p_0(x_0)-\int_0^T\nabla_H\!\cdot v_H(x_t,t)\,\mathrm{d}t,
+ \qquad x_0=\phi_T^{-1}(x).
+ \label{eq:ffjord}
+\end{equation}
+Here $p_0$ is a normalised Gaussian on $H$. This is an ideal clamped-flow density; it is not the
+joint CTMC sampler's conditional likelihood. History-dependent self-conditioning, guidance and
+nonsmooth retractions require separate treatment.
 
-\paragraph{Energy-targeted samplers, and where our objective comes from.} Samplers that target
-$e^{-E/kT}$ directly \citep{noe2019boltzmann,tbg,sbg,arbg,adjointsampling,idem,ewfm} obtain
-equilibrium densities; mass-covering correction by annealed importance sampling \citep{fab}
-addresses the cross-group failure that \Cref{cor:basins} formalises, potential-score matching
-\citep{psm} supervises with the potential's gradient instead of its value, and \citet{idflow} drive
-a predictor--refiner with an \emph{internal, learned} energy instead of an external one. Each takes
-the system, the alphabet or the molecular graph as given: Adjoint Sampling \citep{adjointsampling},
-the closest use of a universal potential as a training signal, trains against an eSEN energy but
-samples conformers of a \emph{given} molecular graph. They therefore solve a stronger sampling
-problem --- equilibrium, not ordering --- on a state space that is narrower and fixed in advance.
-Our value term is inherited from this literature and is not claimed as new:
-$\Var[\log q_\theta + E/kT]$ is the \emph{log-variance divergence} named by \citet{vargrad}, placed
-in a path-space family by \citet{nusken2021pathspace}, given an optimal-control reading by
-\citet{berner2024optimalcontrol} and established for diffusion samplers by
-\citet{richter2024improvedsampling}; that a variance annihilates the normalising constant, and that
-the divergence may be estimated off-policy under an arbitrary reference measure
-\citep{sendera2024offpolicy}, are structural properties of that family, and we claim neither.
+\subsection{The grouped objective}
+\label{sec:method:obj}
+The BGFM loss family is $\Ls_{\rm FM}+\lambda_1\Ls_{\rm force}+\lambda_2\Ls_{\rm energy}$
+with an optional anchor; the value-only experiments set $\lambda_1=\lambda_3=0$ and
+$\lambda_E=\lambda_2=3\times10^{-5}$. The intended energy objective is
+\begin{equation}
+ \boxed{\quad\Ls_{\rm ours}=\Ls_{\rm FM}+\lambda_E\Ls_{\rm value},\qquad
+ \Ls_{\rm value}=\frac1M\sum_m\Var_k[\ell_\theta(x_{m,k}\mid c_m)+E_{m,k}/kT].\quad}
+ \label{eq:lvalue}
+\end{equation}
+Ideally $\ell_\theta=\log q_T$; the archived runs instead use the scalar
+$\widehat\ell_\theta$ defined below. The labels are pre-computed on $K$ perturbed geometries per
+parent. Parent-specific normalisers cancel because a variance is invariant to additive constants;
+a variance pooled over different compositions would retain their different offsets. This explains
+grouping, but does not identify cross-group mass.
 
-\paragraph{The intersection.} Existing de novo generators decide what molecule and what geometry to
-produce, but do not test the tractable density they define against a transferable physical energy;
-energy-targeted samplers test or optimise exactly that relation, but assume the molecular system or
-the graph. This paper works at the intersection, asking what group-wise energy \emph{values} from a
-universal potential do to the density of a generator that is still choosing its molecule. To our
-knowledge the combination studied here --- an external universal potential supervising the
-tractable density of a bond-free de novo flow --- has not been evaluated in this form.
-\Cref{app:related} gives the formal setting and the fuller discussion.
+\subsection{What the archived experiments actually optimise}
+\label{sec:method:cancel}
+\label{sec:method:impl}
+The archived estimator takes $u_\theta(x,t)=D_\theta(x,t)$ directly, uses reverse Euler steps
+$x\leftarrow x-\Delta t\,u_\theta(x,t_k)$ at midpoint \emph{times}, and subtracts the accumulated
+ambient divergence from a prior formula with dimension $3(N-1)$. It neither performs
+\eqref{eq:headvelocity} nor projects the differentiated head onto $H$. We call its scalar output
+$\widehat\ell_\theta$. A midpoint time with a start-of-step state is not a second-order midpoint
+solver. Replacing the step count therefore tests the legacy readout's sensitivity, not the
+correctness of its interpretation as a generator likelihood.
 
